Q: What is a Proxy in Spring?

In Spring AOP, a **proxy** is an intermediate object between the client and the real bean (target).
It intercepts method calls and can run extra logic before/after/around the real method.

Client → Proxy → Target bean

────────────────────────────────────────
1️⃣ Simple definition

A proxy is a wrapper Spring creates around a bean to add cross-cutting behavior
(logging, security, transactions, caching, retries) without changing business code.

────────────────────────────────────────
2️⃣ Real-life analogy

Client → Assistant → CEO

Assistant can check permission, log meeting, schedule time.
Proxy does the same for method calls.

────────────────────────────────────────
3️⃣ Without proxy

userService.createUser();
→ Client → UserService (direct) → no automatic extra behavior

────────────────────────────────────────
4️⃣ With Spring AOP proxy

Client → UserServiceProxy → Aspect advice → UserService.createUser()

Example aspect:
@Aspect
@Component
public class LoggingAspect {
    @Before("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
    public void log() {
        System.out.println("Method called");
    }
}

────────────────────────────────────────
5️⃣ Where Spring uses proxies

- @Transactional
- @Async
- @Cacheable
- @Retry
- @PreAuthorize / method security
- Custom @Aspect advice
- Some lazy bean scenarios

Important consequence:
Self-invocation (`this.method()`) bypasses the proxy → advice may not run.
See: `Why can self-invocation bypass Spring AOP`

────────────────────────────────────────
6️⃣ How Spring creates proxies (next question)

| Type              | When                                      | Based on        |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------- | --------------- |
| JDK Dynamic Proxy | bean implements interface(s) (default preference historically) | java.lang.reflect.Proxy |
| CGLIB Proxy       | concrete class (no interface / or forced) | subclass of target |

Details: `How does Spring create proxies internally` / `Types of proxy...`

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview one-liner

“A Spring proxy wraps the target bean and intercepts calls so AOP can apply transactions, security, caching, logging, etc., before or after the real method.”
